NAME
       sshd_config - settings of the OpenSSH server

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/ssh/sshd_config
       /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf

DESCRIPTION
       sshd, the SSH server, reads its settings from /etc/ssh/sshd_config.
       On RHEL 9 that file starts with "Include /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf",
       so the files in that directory are read first, in name order. For each
       keyword, the FIRST value found wins. That means a setting in a
       drop-in file (such as 50-redhat.conf) beats the same keyword further
       down in sshd_config. Put your own settings in a file that sorts
       first, such as /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-lab.conf, or edit the
       keyword in sshd_config when no drop-in file sets it. Look for the
       keyword in all the files first:

           grep -ri permitrootlogin /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/

       When root logins were allowed during installation, the installer
       wrote 01-permitrootlogin.conf with "PermitRootLogin yes", which wins
       over a later file such as 10-lab.conf.

       Each line is a keyword and its value(s); keywords are not case
       sensitive, values often are. # starts a comment. The commented-out
       lines in the shipped file show the built-in defaults.

       After a change, apply it with systemctl reload sshd (connections
       already open stay open), then check systemctl status sshd and test a
       new login from another terminal before you close the current one.

KEYWORDS
       Port N
              The port to listen on (22). Several Port lines are allowed. A
              new port also needs an SELinux label (semanage port -a -t
              ssh_port_t -p tcp N) and a firewall opening.

       ListenAddress ADDRESS
              The local addresses to listen on (all by default).

       PermitRootLogin yes|no|prohibit-password|forced-commands-only
              Whether root may log in over ssh. prohibit-password (the RHEL 9
              default) allows root with keys only; no forbids root
              completely.

       PasswordAuthentication yes|no
              Whether passwords are accepted. no allows only keys (and other
              methods); set up and test keys first, or you may lock yourself
              out.

       PubkeyAuthentication yes|no
              Whether key logins are accepted (yes).

       AuthorizedKeysFile PATH
              Where the public keys allowed for an account are
              (.ssh/authorized_keys, relative to the home directory).

       AllowUsers USER..., DenyUsers USER...
              Only these users may log in, or these may not. Patterns like
              student@192.168.50.* are allowed.

       AllowGroups GROUP..., DenyGroups GROUP...
              The same for groups.

       PermitEmptyPasswords yes|no
              Whether accounts with an empty password may log in (no).

       X11Forwarding yes|no
              Whether graphical programs may be shown on the client.

       ClientAliveInterval N, ClientAliveCountMax N
              Check idle clients every N seconds; drop them after that many
              unanswered checks.

       MaxAuthTries N
              Login attempts allowed per connection (6).

       Banner FILE
              Text shown before login.

       Match User NAME | Group NAME | Address PATTERN
              The lines that follow apply only to matching connections, up
              to the next Match line or the end of the file.

EXAMPLES
       Forbid root logins and password logins, then apply:

           cat > /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-lab.conf <<'EOF'
           PermitRootLogin no
           PasswordAuthentication no
           EOF
           systemctl reload sshd

       Check which files set a keyword:

           grep -ri passwordauthentication /etc/ssh/sshd_config*

       Allow only student and harry to log in:

           AllowUsers student harry

FILES
       /etc/ssh/sshd_config, /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/
              The settings.

       /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key
              The server's host keys.

SEE ALSO
       sshd(8), ssh_config(5), ssh(1), semanage(8), firewall-cmd(1)
